  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 254 AM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nNever underestimate the power of warm air advection, and apparently, \nthe same goes for moisture advection. Obs Tuesday showed dew points \ntoping 80 degrees in several areas in part because a boundary from \nTuesday morning convection regressed a little further northward than \ninitially anticipated. Showers and cloud cover also dissipated \nslightly faster opening up the opportunity for temperatures to warm \nslightly higher, especially across far northern MO.\n\nThe overall weather pattern remains consistent with what we have \nseen the past few days. Satellite and 500mb analysis show the \neastern quadrants of the ridge centered over northern NM have \nretreated further west. This has opened up the flow for shortwaves \nacross the area as well as displace potential ridge running \nthunderstorm complexes more westward. A surface high descending from \neast central Canada across the region works to keep these waves west \nof the area overnight although some models have been extending \nprecipitation chances are far east as the MO/KS state line early \nthis morning; therefore, have kept a few PoPs across the far west of \nthe area. Some storms have formed over NE KS as of writing and we \nwill have to continue monitoring them and the environment to see how \nthey evolve through the early morning hours. Additional shortwaves \nare expected later this afternoon and evening which may initiate \nsome isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms. Placement of \nthese storms remains a little uncertain as they are expected to be \nmore pulse like in nature. Models scatter these showers across the \narea, but most show higher probabilities in far NW MO. However, if \nthe high is able to build in more substantially, chances for showers \nand storms later today lessen. \n\nChances for showers and storms increase through the day Thursday as \nthe gradient between a leeward low in western KS and a surface high \nacross the southern Great Lakes compresses. This entices low level \nconvergence and lift leading to the more widespread showers and \nstorms. Convective variable remain rather muted at this time \nlimiting concerns for strong to severe storms, but instability of \n~2000 J/kg with bulk shear around 25 knots does present a marginal \nrisk for a strong to severe storm.\n\nAs the weekend approaches, a substantial shortwave trough descends \nfrom interior Canada and looks to bring greater chances for showers \nand storms as well as cooler conditions across the area. \nAcceleration of low level flow ahead of the trough quickly pushes \nwarm air and moisture into the region. This pushes PWAT values up to \n1.5 to 2 inches which presents and environment favorable for heavy \nrainfall. Large scale instability and lapse rates are somewhat \nlimited curtailing much of the strong to severe concerns at this \ntime. As storms depart Friday evening, cooler air pushes in behind \nand highs Saturday only reach the low to mid 80s. Early next week \nanother ridge settles in across the Great Lakes which pushes \ntemperatures back upward toward seasonal normals. Extended guidance \nshows the synoptic ridges across the southern CONUS weakening \nenabling the northern jet stream to sag southward potentially \nbringing a more dynamic pattern further south into the CONUS.",
